# Week C (Mon 9 to Sun 15 Nov 2026) voiceovers. Cloud and identity.
# Numbers spelled out; avoid symbols the narrator would read oddly.
S = {
# MON 9
"09-0700": "Thousands of company files were sitting in a public storage bucket, readable by anyone with the link. No hack needed. Public buckets usually happen by accident. A quick fix during testing, a website asset folder, or an old bucket created before safer defaults existed. Newer buckets block public access by default, but older ones and one-off exceptions remain. Turn on public access blocks for the whole account. Share files with short-lived signed links instead. And scan for anything public, every day.",
"09-0900": "Your cloud provider secures the cloud. You secure everything you put in it. That is the shared responsibility model, and it is where many breaches start. The provider handles the data centres, the hardware and the core services. You own your identities and permissions, your configuration, your network rules, your data and your code. A provider can't stop you from making a bucket public or giving everyone admin. Know which side of the line each control sits on, and who in your team owns it.",
"09-1300": "An access key created three years ago still works today, with the same power it had on day one. Long-lived keys get copied into scripts, laptops and chat messages, and nobody remembers where. Short-lived credentials expire on their own. Use roles for workloads, so servers and functions get temporary credentials automatically. Use single sign-on for people, so nobody needs a personal access key. Then find the keys that remain, rotate them, and delete the ones nobody uses.",
"09-1500": "What does a cloud security engineer actually do all day? Less hacking than you'd think, more building. Reviewing infrastructure changes before they ship. Tuning alerts so the important ones get seen. Fixing permissions that grew too wide. Helping developers set up secure defaults, so the safe way is the easy way. And answering the question, is this exposed? The best ones can read infrastructure code, explain risk in plain words, and automate the boring checks.",
"09-1700": "One firewall rule left your database open to the whole internet. Rules that allow any address are meant for public websites, not databases, remote desktop or admin ports. Yet they appear all the time, added during a late-night fix and never removed. Automated scanners find them within minutes. Allow only the addresses that need access. Put admin access behind a VPN or a managed access service. And alert whenever a rule opens a sensitive port to everyone.",
"09-1900": "How old is the oldest access key in your cloud account? A week? A year? Older than some of your employees? Most teams don't know until they check. Check today, and tell me the number.",
# TUE 10
"10-0700": "Your deployment pipeline can change production. So anyone who controls the pipeline controls production. Pipelines often hold powerful cloud credentials, run third-party actions, and build code from any branch. An attacker who can edit a workflow file, or poison a dependency, inherits all of that. Protect the main branch and require reviews. Pin third-party actions to exact versions. Separate build from deploy. And give the deploy step only the permissions it truly needs.",
"10-0900": "What if your pipeline needed no stored cloud secrets at all? With workload identity, it doesn't. Your CI system proves who it is to your cloud provider using a short-lived signed token, and gets temporary credentials for one job. Nothing long-lived to leak, nothing to rotate. Most major CI platforms and clouds support it today. Set it up, scope the trust to one repository and one branch, and delete the old stored keys.",
"10-1300": "Kubernetes has hundreds of settings. Three matter most when you start. One. Access control. Give people and workloads only the permissions they need, and avoid cluster admin for everyday work. Two. Pod security. Don't run containers as privileged or as root, and block access to the host. Three. Network policies. By default, every pod can talk to every other pod. Restrict that to what each service needs. Get these three right before anything fancy.",
"10-1500": "Azure, AWS or Google Cloud. Does the one you learn decide your career? Less than you think. The ideas transfer. Identity, networking, logging, encryption, and least privilege work the same way everywhere, just with different names. Pick the cloud your target employers use. Go deep on one, then learn how the others name the same things. Employers hire for understanding, not for memorised console menus.",
"10-1700": "Your container runs as root, on a base image last updated two years ago. Both are easy to miss and easy to fix. If an attacker breaks into a container running as root, they start with the most power. Old base images carry known vulnerabilities into every deploy. Run as a non-root user. Use small, regularly updated base images. Scan images in your pipeline. And rebuild on a schedule, not only when the code changes.",
"10-1900": "Who can deploy to production in your team? Everyone? Only a pipeline? One person with a laptop? The answer tells you a lot about your risk. What is it for you?",
# WED 11
"11-0700": "Single sign-on means one login for everything. Get it wrong, and it means one failure for everything. Common mistakes. Apps that still allow a local password alongside single sign-on. Not checking which identity provider signed the response. Matching users by email address, so a different account with the same email gets in. And sessions in apps that outlive the main login. Enforce single sign-on everywhere, validate every response, and link accounts by a stable ID.",
"11-0900": "They left the company three months ago. Their access didn't. Joiner, mover, leaver is the identity lifecycle, and every stage leaks. Joiners get copied permissions from a colleague. Movers keep their old access when they change teams. Leavers keep accounts in tools outside single sign-on. Drive access from your HR system. Remove old access when someone moves. Disable everything on the last day. And review who has access to what, every quarter.",
"11-1300": "Your users have multi-factor authentication. But some logins skip it entirely. Older protocols, like basic authentication for email clients, can't ask for a second factor. If they're allowed, an attacker with a stolen password walks straight past MFA. Block legacy authentication with conditional access policies. Check your sign-in logs for logins using old protocols first, so you don't break anything. Then require MFA, or a compliant device, for every app.",
"11-1500": "Building application security from zero? Don't start with tools. Start with knowing what you have. First, list your applications, who owns them, and which handle money or personal data. Second, fix the basics: secrets out of code, dependency updates, authentication done right. Third, add security checks to the pipeline that developers trust. Fourth, train developers on the bugs you actually find. Then measure. Fewer repeat bugs means it's working.",
"11-1700": "When single sign-on fails, or MFA is broken, how do you get back in? With a break-glass account. An emergency account kept for exactly that moment. Do it safely. Keep two, cloud-only, not tied to any one person or system. Protect them with strong phishing-resistant keys, stored securely. Exclude them only from the policies that could lock you out. Alert every time they're used. And test them every few months, so they work when you need them.",
"11-1900": "When someone leaves your company, how fast is their access removed? Same day, same week, or same month? Be honest. What happens in your team?",
# THU 12
"12-0700": "The account could only read. A few steps later, it was an administrator. That is privilege escalation through role chaining. One role is allowed to assume a second role. The second can pass a role to a new server or function. That server runs with admin rights. No single permission looks dangerous on its own. Map who can assume which roles, and who can pass roles to new resources. Remove the paths nobody needs, and alert on unusual role assumptions.",
"12-0900": "Zero trust in plain words. Being inside the network doesn't make you trusted. In the old model, the office network was the castle, and anything inside the walls was trusted. Today, staff work from anywhere and apps live in the cloud, so the walls don't exist. Zero trust checks every request. Who is this? Is their device healthy? Should they reach this resource, right now? Start with identity and strong authentication, then add device checks and smaller network segments.",
"12-1300": "The worst time to discover you weren't logging is during an incident. Turn these on before you need them. First, your cloud control-plane logs, in every region, so you see who changed what. Second, sign-in logs from your identity provider. Third, network flow logs for important systems. Fourth, access logs for storage holding sensitive data. Send them to a separate account that attackers can't delete from, and keep them for months, not days.",
"12-1500": "What does a real security incident look like when you're on call? Rarely a movie hacker. Usually an alert at an awkward hour that might be nothing. A login from an unusual country. A spike in failed passwords. A key used from a new place. The job is to decide quickly: real or noise? Then contain, gather evidence, and tell the right people. Good runbooks and calm communication matter more than speed typing.",
"12-1700": "Environment variables are convenient. They are not a vault. They show up in crash reports, debug pages, process listings and logs. Anyone who can read the server's configuration can read them. A secrets manager stores secrets encrypted, controls who can read each one, logs every access, and makes rotation simple. Load secrets from the manager at runtime. Keep them out of images and code. And rotate the important ones on a schedule.",
"12-1900": "Where do your production secrets live? A secrets manager, environment variables, a config file, or a shared document somewhere? No judgement. Where are yours?",
# FRI 13
"13-0700": "Friday the thirteenth. Here are the unluckiest cloud mistakes. Except they aren't luck. A storage bucket left public. A database open to the internet. An access key pushed to a public repository. Logging switched off to save money. An admin account without MFA. Every one is a configuration choice, and every one has a quick fix. Bad luck is when something happens you couldn't prevent. These, you can.",
"13-0900": "What if the open database never reached production, because the pull request caught it? Infrastructure as code scanning checks your Terraform, CloudFormation or Kubernetes files before they deploy. Public storage, open ports, missing encryption, wide permissions, all flagged in review. Start with a standard rule set, and block only the serious issues at first, so developers trust it. Fixing a line in a pull request is far cheaper than fixing production.",
"13-1300": "You shared a file with a link that would expire. Then someone set the expiry to a week. Or a year. Signed links are a good way to share private files, because nobody needs to make the storage public. But a long-lived link works for anyone who has it, forwarded or leaked. Keep expiry short, minutes for downloads. Generate links only after checking the user may see the file. And never sign links with a powerful long-lived key.",
"13-1500": "What does a cloud security interview actually test? Usually three things. First, fundamentals. How identity and permissions work, how networks are segmented, how data is encrypted. Second, scenarios. Here is an exposed bucket, or a leaked key. What do you do, in what order? Third, communication. Can you explain a risk to an engineer and a manager? Practise talking through real scenarios out loud. Certifications get you in the room. Reasoning gets the offer.",
"13-1700": "Sometimes the first sign of an attack is the bill. Stolen cloud credentials are often used to mine cryptocurrency, on the biggest machines available, in regions you never use. The data was safe. The invoice wasn't. Set budget alerts and anomaly detection on your accounts. Restrict which regions and machine types can be used. And treat a sudden spending spike as a security alert, not just a finance question.",
"13-1900": "What's the biggest cloud surprise you've ever had? A bill, an exposed resource, a service you forgot existed? Share it, so others don't repeat it.",
# SAT 14
"14-0700": "Someone else is serving content on your subdomain, and your customers trust it. That is a subdomain takeover. Your DNS still points to a cloud resource you deleted, like a storage site or an app service. An attacker creates a resource with the same name, and your subdomain now serves their page. When you delete a resource, delete its DNS record first. Review DNS records for anything pointing to services you no longer own.",
"14-0900": "Anyone can send an email that says it's from your domain, unless you stop them. Three records do the work. SPF lists the servers allowed to send your mail. DKIM signs your messages, so changes can be detected. DMARC tells receivers what to do when a message fails those checks, and sends you reports. Start DMARC at monitor mode, fix your legitimate senders, then move to quarantine, and finally reject.",
"14-1300": "At midnight, the certificate expired, and the whole app went down. No attacker needed. Certificate lifetimes are getting shorter. Since March this year, the maximum is two hundred days, and it keeps shrinking, to forty seven days by twenty twenty nine. Manual renewals won't keep up. Automate renewal for every certificate, including internal ones. Monitor expiry dates with alerts weeks ahead. And keep a list of every certificate and who owns it.",
"14-1500": "Want to learn cloud security hands on? Build a small lab, in your own account, on a budget. Set a spending alert first. Deploy a simple app with storage and a database. Then misconfigure it on purpose. Make a bucket public, open a port, give a role too much power. Use free tools to scan your account and find what you broke. Fix each issue, and write up what you learned. Then delete everything, so the bill stays small.",
"14-1700": "Your admin panel was meant for staff. It was also open to the whole internet. Exposed admin consoles, like database dashboards, monitoring tools and content management backends, are some of the first things attackers scan for. Even with a password, they invite guessing and exploits. Keep admin interfaces off the public internet. Put them behind a VPN or an identity-aware proxy. Require MFA. And scan your own address ranges to find the ones you forgot.",
"14-1900": "Is your DMARC policy set to none, quarantine, or reject? None only monitors. Reject actually stops spoofed email. Check your domain today. Where are you?",
# SUN 15
"15-0700": "The attacker didn't just encrypt your servers. They deleted your backups first. Many ransomware groups go for backups before anything else, because backups are how you recover without paying. Keep backups in a separate account, with separate credentials. Make them immutable, so nobody, not even an admin, can delete them before a set date. Restrict who can change backup policies. And alert on any attempt to delete or shorten retention.",
"15-0900": "One cloud account for everything means one mistake can reach everything. Separate accounts create walls. Production, development, logging and security each get their own account. Developers experimenting in development can't touch production. Logs live where attackers in production can't delete them. Use your provider's organization features to apply guardrails across all accounts at once. Small accounts, small blast radius.",
"15-1300": "A service account with full access, created by someone who left two years ago. Nobody knows what uses it. So nobody dares turn it off. Service accounts outnumber people in most companies, and they rarely get reviewed. Give every service account an owner and a purpose. Scope its permissions to that purpose. Use short-lived credentials where possible. And when nobody claims one, disable it, watch what breaks, and decide.",
"15-1500": "The fastest way to get better at security is to teach someone else. Mentoring juniors makes you explain things clearly, and spot the gaps in your own knowledge. Start small. Pair on a code review. Walk them through a finding, from discovery to fix. Share the resources that helped you. Let them present something to the team. One person you help today becomes someone who helps others tomorrow.",
"15-1700": "Passwordless for staff sounds like a big project. Start small, with the accounts that matter most. Your administrators. Cloud console access, identity admin, finance systems. Give them phishing-resistant passkeys or security keys, and require them for those roles. Then expand to everyone with access to sensitive data. Then the rest of the company. Each step removes passwords that can be phished, reused or guessed.",
"15-1900": "If you had one week to improve security at your company, what would you fix first? Not a wish list. One thing. What would it be, and why?",
}
